\description{
PTM-PTM pairs within one dataset: two modifications identified together on one peptidoform (physical), and two sites whose occupancies rise and fall together across runs (co-varying), with Benjamini-Hochberg q-values.

Two kinds of evidence, which you choose between with \code{type}:
}
\details{
- \strong{physical} (\code{PtmPairEngine.Physical}): both modifications were identified on one
  peptidoform, so on one molecule. \code{statistic} is the median co-occupancy over the runs where both
  were quantified. There is no test.
- \strong{covarying} (\code{PtmPairEngine.CoVarying}): the two sites' occupancies rise and fall together (or
  oppositely) across runs. \code{statistic} is Spearman's rho on the runs where both are quantified,
  with a Benjamini-Hochberg \code{q} per family. Floors, undetected cells and, by default, ceilings are
  left out: two unrelated sites both read 1 in low-load runs, which would correlate them through
  detection alone.

Occupancy is computed inside by the same mzLib call \code{\link{ptmqtl_occupancy}} makes.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_ptm_pairs}:

